<figure class="case-lead-image"><img src="/img/Threshold/policy-report-title-slide-housing-advocacy.webp" width="1920" height="1080" alt="Threshold presentation title: The housing failure."></figure>

<section class="case-summary">
  <div><p class="eyebrow">The problem</p><h2>Informing an audience was not enough. The material needed to build a case.</h2></div>
  <div class="prose"><p>This self-initiated example explores advocacy communication around housing. The source material contains competing explanations, long-term data and policy choices. The challenge is to establish a position without losing the evidence behind it.</p><p>The sequence moves from problem framing, through a memorable comparison and supporting data, towards a practical alternative.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="decision-band"><span>01</span><div><p class="eyebrow">The decision</p><h2>Make the scale of the failure visible before explaining its causes.</h2></div></section>

<section class="case-gallery" data-gallery>
  <figure class="gallery-wide"><img src="/img/Threshold/advocacy-data-comparison-infographic-presentation-slide.webp" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy" alt="A geometric comparison between social housing built in 1980 and 2024."><figcaption><strong>Magnitude before detail.</strong> The triangle device makes the scale differential visible before the numbers are read.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure><img src="/img/Threshold/problem-statement-narrative-framework-presentation-slide.webp" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy" alt="Problem statement slide explaining why incentives favour waiting over building."><figcaption><strong>Frame the debate.</strong> A short sequence of statements establishes the argument before evidence is introduced.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure><img src="/img/Threshold/policy-data-area-chart-presentation-slide-layout.webp" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy" alt="Area chart comparing permissions granted with homes completed."><figcaption><strong>Test the assumption.</strong> Longitudinal data makes the structural gap visible; the side panel explains what it means.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure><img src="/img/Threshold/regional-demographic-data-table-presentation-slide.webp" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy" alt="Regional demographic table and geometric map."><figcaption><strong>Add the necessary depth.</strong> Detailed evidence remains available without disrupting the main narrative.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure><img src="/img/Threshold/circular-development-model-infographic-presentation-slide.webp" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy" alt="Circular alternative development model diagram."><figcaption><strong>Move from diagnosis to action.</strong> The final framework gives the audience a model to discuss.</figcaption></figure>
</section>

<section class="case-outcome"><div><p class="eyebrow">The practical output</p><h2>A presentation argument with editable charts, diagrams and a clear sequence.</h2></div><p>This is self-initiated work, created to demonstrate our method rather than represent a client engagement. It shows how our <a href="/services/presentation-design/">presentation design</a> moves from evidence and structure to a finished advocacy presentation.</p></section>

